00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:53.920
There's no jobs.


00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:55.730
Indiana has
an unemployment rate


00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:58.170
for, um, like
high school diploma,


00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:01.140
no college,
of, like, 26%.


00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.140
Oh, wow.


00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:03.640
Yeah, it's really bad,


00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:04.640
and so I tried
to get a job,


00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.150
I tried to get a job,
I couldn't.


00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:09.070
I couldn't get a job,
couldn't get a job,


00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.160
and so I read an article,


00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:14.600
said there's boatloads
of work in North Dakota.


00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.110
Boatloads.


00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.530
My dad said, "Well, um...


00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.450
"I can get some money
from grandma, and I'll
loan you some.


00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.950
I'll get you
a one-way ticket there."


00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.840
You know?
And it's just--


00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:30.370
It's been trial after trial.


00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:33.420
And I'm just at
the end of my rope.


00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.920
You can get a job,


00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.050
but you gotta translate
into housing.


00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.640
And that's the--
that's the killer.


00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.510
Rents have tripled
and quadrupled.


00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:46.030
People who have lived here
all their lives happen to leave.


00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:49.020
And other people
who own property


00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.520
just don't like the changes
and they're leaving.


00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.030
They're selling it.


00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:53.530
But everything's
expensive.


00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:54.990
It's very expensive
right now, you know?


00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:59.480
So it is legal to sleep
in your car in Williston,


00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.480
but you can only sleep
where people give you
permission to sleep,


00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.380
and there's not very many places
that do.


00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:06.740
Anyway, so--so you can stay
for a month,


00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:09.540
and unless there's a problem,
we do ask a gift.


00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.540
We don't charge this.


00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:12.840
We're not a shelter.


00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:14.160
This is a place
for men to get jobs.


00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:16.170
Now real quick I'm going to run
through these rules, ok?


00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:17.470
First we're at church.


00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:18.560
If you're not going to church
someplace else,


00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.050
I'd like you to come to church.


00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:21.050
I think it's only polite, ok?


00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.560
I don't know what
your church background is.


00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:23.560
We might have a chance
to do that,


00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.150
but we do background checks
here, ok?


00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.420
No weapons, no fighting,
no profanity.


00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:28.740
You want to stay
on my good side,


00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:30.230
don't spill coffee
on the carpet.


00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:33.730
Drives me crazy when people
spill coffee on the carpet,


00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:36.230
because--because
that becomes evidence.


00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.250
"There's a man staying
in the building,"


00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:39.600
you know what I'm saying?


00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.030
800 or 900 guys
come through this building.


00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:43.870
And you know it's a struggle
we've got, it really is.


00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:44.860
Did you get
yours signed?


00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:46.360
Oh, no, I haven't.


00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:47.360
Ok. So welcome to
Williston, gentlemen.


00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:49.920
It's gonna be a trip.


00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.050
What a profound thing.


00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:00.510
That we have people literally
walking up to our door


00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:05.960
from all over the world,
saying, "Can you help me?"


00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:11.550
WOMAN: We are on our way
to Williston, North Dakota,


00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.400
hopefully to find jobs.


00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:15.220
MAN: We're here
in DeLand, Florida,


00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:18.650
destination, uh, North Dakota.


00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:22.120
DIFFERENT MAN: I got
my co-pilot, Artie.


00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:23.990
Say hi, Artie.


00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:35.160
DIFFERENT MAN:
Gonna go out there and try
to find an oil-rigging job.


00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:36.460
I spoke to a buddy of mine,


00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.250
and he said he's making
6 figures out there.


00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:50.390
I'll find a job within an hour
or two, he said.


00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:53.430
JAY REINKE:
I have to respect these men


00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:56.680
because I've asked myself,
"What would I do


00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:59.600
if I got off a bus
and I had to find a job?"


00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:03.940
And these men do it.


00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:12.920
MAN:
I don't know about going back
home, but this is where it's at.


00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:16.180
$15 an hour is minimum wage
up here.


00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:17.950
15 bucks.


00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.430
You're not gonna find this
nowhere in America.


00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:22.920
People with 10 felonies
is getting 100 grand a year


00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:24.890
just 'cause you tough enough.


00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:32.060
I really have no desire
to leave sunny Arizona


00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.050
to go up to the cold
of North Dakota,


00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:37.600
but I don't have
a whole lot of choice.


00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:41.150
MAN: This may be some point
of hope for everybody.


00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:44.490
DIFFERENT MAN:
We've already rented out
a travel trailer


00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:46.980
because we know that
space up there is limited.


00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:57.380
I can't afford to live.
Plain and simple.


00:06:57.380 --> 00:06:59.720
JAY:
It's very hard to have someone


00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:03.160
stand in your office
and say, "I have nowhere to go."


00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:06.010
And to say,
"Sorry, I can't help."


00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.920
I've got floor space.


00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:26.350
[Singing indistinctly]


00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:37.760
REINKE:
The Christian ethic is now,


00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:41.130
who is my neighbor
and how do I serve him?


00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.540
People without a place to sleep.


00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.870
There's a neighbor in Williston
looking at how to serve him.


00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:51.190
Not only are these men
my neighbor,


00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:56.220
but people who don't want them
here are also my neighbor.


00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:01.360
The whole overnight thing
wasn't really planned.


00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:05.030
The congregation never really
did decide to do this,


00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:07.980
and that's probably
one of the chief criticisms


00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:10.360
that I have to bear.


00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:24.160
WOMAN: It started out
with one guy about a year ago,


00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:27.130
and it's just kind of gotten
to be a lot more.


00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:29.580
It's not something we really
just talked about and said,


00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:31.100
"Oh, yeah, why don't we
do this."


00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:32.550
It just kind of happened.


00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:37.100
People come and they have needs,
and you want to help them.


00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:39.590
When you start getting
too many people in the building,


00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:43.490
it just--it's a little bit
harder to maintain,


00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:46.900
The organization and keep things
clean and everything.


00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:49.600
And that can be a concern
for the congregation.


00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:52.700
WOMAN: It's put a strain
on our family life


00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:55.280
because for the most part,
my husband's had to be here


00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:57.280
every night and every morning.


00:08:57.280 --> 00:08:59.410
WOMAN:
Which is kind of a bummer,


00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:03.010
but you know, he tries to make
as much time as possible


00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:05.870
to serve everywhere
he needs to serve.


00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:09.420
ANDREA REINKE:
Everybody who comes here
says, "You don't know.


00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:12.270
Everybody's talking about
coming to Williston."


00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:17.510
Hopefully at some point
we'll transition back
to how it used to be.


00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:30.730
JAY: I don't know
of any other parallel.


00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:32.310
There's been boom towns,


00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:34.290
but it hasn't had this kind
of depressed economy


00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:37.530
that's putting the pressure
for people to go somewhere


00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:39.500
the way this one is.


00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:45.130
It's happening in
a relatively small community,


00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:50.300
so the impact of the migration,
it's just evident everywhere.


00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:57.350
Thanks very much.
Ok, we'll see you.


00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.020
MAN:
I'm not sure if it's legal.


00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:16.910
I don't know if you are even
aware of some of this.


00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:19.330
We're just trying to make our
point why we need
to do something.


00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:21.330
This is a great example
of what we're seeing


00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:22.860
in single family
residential areas.


00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:26.210
This area here
is near the baseball field.


00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:28.170
We've got 3 campers
in a backyard.


00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:30.950
One's a motor home,
the other two are pull-behinds.


00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:34.130
That's not acceptable.
That's not a safe environment.


00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:36.520
This is a health concern.
It's a safety concern.


00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:38.630
And that is why the city
needs to do something.


00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:42.380
JAY:
Tonight's city council meeting
will be the first formal vote


00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:44.900
on an RV ban.


00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:48.890
It will mean that
RVs and campers


00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:50.770
cannot have people
living in them


00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:53.570
for more than 28 days
in a year.


00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:54.860
That scares me.


00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:57.370
I've been in town a long time.


00:10:57.370 --> 00:11:02.380
I hate to take the role
of the antagonist,


00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:04.320
but I think somebody
needs to turn some rocks over


00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:06.320
and say, "So what is community
in this place?


00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:07.330
What are we gonna do here?"


00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:10.250
MAN: We asked for public input.


00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:12.420
Pastor Reinke.


00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:14.170
Thank you, Mayor Poser.


00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:16.510
I would speak against
the adoption of this
ordinance.


00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:19.840
I believe that Williston
needs to take a step


00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:22.840
that says the people
arriving on our doorstep


00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:24.840
are gifts to us,


00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.350
and though there's a burden
in receiving them as a gift,


00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:29.360
to welcome them.


00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:34.270
I also believe that
by passing such an ordinance


00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:37.320
we risk serving fear,


00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:39.820
the fear of the people
coming at us,


00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:43.540
and the last thing we need to do
is to serve our fear.


00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:45.410
Ok, thank you, Pastor.


00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:53.620
WOMAN: So our top story,
the FBI says it believes


00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:55.920
it has uncovered the body of
missing teacher Sherry Arnold.


00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:58.770
MAN: And federal
and multi-state law enforcement


00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:01.090
discovered a woman's body
east of Williston,
North Dakota,


00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:02.940
some time yesterday.


00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:04.260
The 43-year-old
high school math teacher


00:12:04.270 --> 00:12:07.530
went for a jog
and never returned.


00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:08.940
MAN: This morning investigators
say the know a lot more


00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:10.940
about the two men they believe
kidnapped her.


00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:12.770
They may driven through


00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:15.070
the popular high school
teacher's hometown


00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:18.310
hoping to find jobs
in the booming oil industry.


00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:20.210
Arnold's disappearance
is fueling tensions


00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:23.280
between locals and the flood
of out-of-towners.


00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:27.580
This normally peaceful town
is living in fear.


00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:29.790
JAY: The Sherry Arnold case
was huge,


00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:33.040
and it made us suspicious
in ways...


00:12:33.550 --> 00:12:35.970
that I don't think
we would have been.


00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:40.220
It really does intensify
the fear.


00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:43.550
Everybody's afraid
of the newcomer.


00:12:47.510 --> 00:12:52.100
Very specifically
the overnighters are not welcome
in this part of our community,


00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:58.190
and what scares me is that
good men are vulnerable.


00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:01.490
We've had people sleeping
in our parking lot


00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:03.910
for sometimes over a month.


00:13:03.910 --> 00:13:05.910
They're waiting to come
in the building.


00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:09.030
Now, something we have to
really, really be mindful of,


00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:12.500
people, when they see
the new men in town,


00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:15.840
they wonder--
and we're a church,


00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:17.790
this is a place
where sinners should come--


00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:20.960
but it is
a very difficult PR issue,


00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:23.540
because from
a neighbor's perspective,


00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:25.440
they could perceive us
as harboring somebody


00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:27.930
who could hurt their child.


00:13:27.940 --> 00:13:32.230
If you feel like people regard
you with a little bit of...


00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:36.520
distance, love 'em anyway.


00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:37.780
Keep trying to build
a community here,


00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:39.790
try and be part
of the community.


00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:50.200
MAN: "By the embrace
of our Lord and savior
Jesus Christ,


00:13:50.210 --> 00:13:52.580
"Concordia Lutheran Church
would like to welcome you.


00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:54.420
"While we open
our doors to you,


00:13:54.410 --> 00:13:56.880
"there are some
very important ground rules
that must be followed.


00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:58.960
"This building is
the house of our Lord


00:13:58.970 --> 00:13:59.970
and must be respected
at all times."


00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:01.260
Absolutely.


00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:03.550
I ask that you try to field


00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:05.050
anything you can
through me first


00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:07.560
and try to tie up
as little of the pastor's time


00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:09.570
as you can.


00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:11.060
He still has a congregation
in the church


00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:12.560
and a lot of stuff
on his plate


00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:13.810
that he still does.


00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:15.680
When I came here,
I had a closed heart.


00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:17.680
I wasn't gonna let
anybody close to me.


00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:19.680
And ever since coming here
and listening to the pastor,


00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:21.190
I mean, it's just
I got ripped away.


00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:22.690
That's awesome.


00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:24.190
I've turned down
two $28 an hour jobs.


00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:25.690
What?


00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:27.270
Just to stay here
and help.


00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:28.790
Now we do have a lady here
that does back ground checks.


00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:30.780
Mm-hmm. I mean,
I've never done
anything bad,


00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:32.780
but there's a couple
of things on there.


00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:34.280
I have. I've done
a lot of bad stuff,


00:14:34.790 --> 00:14:37.090
and so it's all good.


00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:38.380
ALAN MEZO, VOICE-OVER:
I've overcome a lot of things


00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:40.250
in my past.


00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:42.050
I was a drunk and a drug addict,


00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:44.090
and you know,
I lived a bad life.


00:14:44.090 --> 00:14:47.130
I spent 16 years of my life
in prison.


00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:49.130
I just didn't care.


00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:51.430
And today I do.


00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:53.730
WOMAN: Jeremy,
were you here
last night?


00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:55.050
MAN: No.
Ok.


00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:56.900
And you are?


00:14:56.890 --> 00:14:58.890
MEZO: The pastor says
he trusts my judgment,


00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:02.390
and if I make a decision,
he'll run with it.


00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:04.910
And that's comforting.


00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:08.780
This is probably
the safest place
in Williston to be right now.


00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:11.410
To be out looking for work
and have your heads down.


00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:13.710
But anytime you're here,
you're safe


00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:16.000
and like to keep it that way.


00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:18.990
That's how we're going to be
able to continue doing this.


00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:20.500
It really meant a lot
to come to a place


00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:22.510
that felt like I was welcome.


00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:24.000
I mean, this is the only place
that I came


00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:25.500
where I was like,
"This is a home.


00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:26.500
"These are good people here.


00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:29.000
I like being..."
you know.


00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:30.850
And that meant a lot to me.


00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:32.060
And if it wasn't for this place,


00:15:32.060 --> 00:15:34.890
we'd pretty much be lost.


00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:42.280
? As long as life endures ?


00:15:42.270 --> 00:15:49.270
? Through many dangers,
toils, and snares ?


00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:55.750
? I have already come ?


00:15:55.750 --> 00:16:03.790
? His grace has brought
me safe thus far ?


00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:09.880
? His grace will lead me home ?


00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:22.280
WOMAN: In church, a lot of times
I feel really angry


00:16:22.280 --> 00:16:23.650
when I look around
and see these guys,


00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:27.230
and I feel upset when I walk in
and my church is messy


00:16:27.730 --> 00:16:30.110
and it smells bad
and there's sand on the pews,


00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:32.080
and they seem disrespectful
in church.


00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:33.980
Their cell phones are going off,


00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:36.880
and their--sometimes
they're talking


00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:38.370
and they're walking in and out,


00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:41.380
and they don't seem
to understand what we're doing


00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:43.890
you know,
and they don't respect it.


00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:48.380
And I get that, but I understand
that's not being very Christian


00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:53.230
and all those kinds of things,
but those are real feelings.


00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:55.560
I don't see that
that much truthfully.


00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:57.560
And I think sometimes the guys
stand around Sunday morning,


00:16:58.060 --> 00:16:59.560
and they're not sure
what to do.


00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:01.060
Do they sit here?


00:17:01.060 --> 00:17:03.060
Do they--You know, what's
the appropriate place to be?


00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:05.080
So they feel
a little bit awkward,


00:17:05.070 --> 00:17:07.850
'cause they realize they're
in someone else's domain.


00:17:07.850 --> 00:17:10.150
If you can't get a job
here in the oil patch,


00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:13.170
and you know,
within the first 48 hours,


00:17:13.160 --> 00:17:16.160
72 hours, you're probably
not going to get a job there.


00:17:16.160 --> 00:17:18.660
So then you're going to be
working McDonald's, Hardee's,


00:17:18.670 --> 00:17:20.010
whatever.


00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:21.750
So we're just going
to contribute


00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:23.750
to that layer
in the community


00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:26.170
that doesn't need to be
contributed to.


00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:27.170
Shelly, I--


00:17:27.670 --> 00:17:28.670
That's a huge concern
of mine.


00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:30.850
Part of my burden is


00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:32.840
I do know
some of these families,


00:17:32.850 --> 00:17:34.850
and I've talked to
some of the wives,


00:17:34.850 --> 00:17:38.190
and they are so grateful.


00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:41.030
How long do we intend
to support them here


00:17:41.020 --> 00:17:42.520
is my question.


00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:44.440
Because they have
no intention


00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:46.720
of building
anything here.


00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:50.540
And so these people come
and they rape, pillage,
and burn,


00:17:50.530 --> 00:17:51.950
and then they leave.


00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:54.360
It's heartbreaking to me


00:17:54.370 --> 00:17:59.250
to see my plains,
my prairies destroyed,


00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:03.240
The wildlife destroyed,
our water destroyed.


00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:09.800
This is not...
my home anymore, you know?


00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:11.800
It's very difficult.


00:18:29.990 --> 00:18:32.000
[Indistinct chatter]


00:18:40.700 --> 00:18:44.590
MAN: I figured it was time
for someone in my family


00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:46.670
to be successful,


00:18:46.670 --> 00:18:49.670
not just another person
in a little, small town


00:18:49.670 --> 00:18:51.590
barely making it.


00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:53.540
And I wanted to
get somewhere quick


00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:57.130
and be able to start a good life
for my family.


00:18:59.270 --> 00:19:01.220
DIFFERENT MAN: Oil mud
is what's in the pipes


00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:03.060
that we clean them out,


00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:05.770
and solvent is what we use
to clean off the pipe filth


00:19:05.770 --> 00:19:07.720
and oil mud.


00:19:07.720 --> 00:19:10.720
Really, really dirty work.


00:19:10.730 --> 00:19:13.200
The solvent just kind of
gets to your skin after a while,


00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:15.710
but they keep telling us


00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:19.200
that it's gonna stop
bothering us after a while.


00:19:24.070 --> 00:19:26.460
Bad things happen out here.


00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:29.330
And that's one nice thing
about this place,


00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:31.660
gonna have people
like Pastor Reinke


00:19:31.670 --> 00:19:36.340
where gives you either a place
to stay or food or whatever.


00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:39.600
I mean, he's not just gonna
let you hang out to dry.


00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:41.970
He'll take you as you are.


00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:43.900
[Indistinct chatter]


00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:02.240
MAN:
I'm a journeyman electrician,


00:20:02.250 --> 00:20:03.750
and I don't know.


00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:07.120
I thought I could come out here,
and if I worked hard


00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:09.010
and I was a hard worker
and I knew what I was doing,


00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:10.500
that I'd get a job pretty easy.


00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:15.370
You know, but I'm seeing now
that it's not that easy.


00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:18.270
Hey, Mr. Eddie Clark,
my name is Michael Batten.


00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:19.680
I want to talk to you.


00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:22.100
If you get time, you think
you could give me a call?


00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:26.100
My number is 912-850-3923.


00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:28.600
And I got your message
about the resume,


00:20:28.610 --> 00:20:30.110
and I'm going to try
to get one together tonight


00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:32.120
to fax to you.


00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:34.110
Thank you .


00:20:35.110 --> 00:20:36.980
JAY, VOICE-OVER:
There are guys that come,


00:20:37.480 --> 00:20:40.360
and I hate to use
the word "failed,"


00:20:40.370 --> 00:20:42.160
but they've not succeeded
as they'd hoped.


00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:44.980
And coming to Williston
represents an opportunity


00:20:44.990 --> 00:20:49.880
to try something again
on a new playing field


00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:52.390
with new opportunities.


00:20:52.630 --> 00:20:53.910
MAN: This is
my resume, ok?


00:20:54.380 --> 00:20:57.910
You can see the format
that I have laid out.


00:20:57.920 --> 00:20:59.230
MICHAEL BATTEN:
I like that.


00:20:59.220 --> 00:21:01.130
So I'll kind of use mine
as a template.


00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:02.650
Ok, good.


00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:04.890
And we'll copy your
information into there.


00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:08.010
I've been
listing the church
as my home address.


00:21:08.010 --> 00:21:09.510
They're not gonna
hire you


00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:11.060
if you appear to be
out of state.


00:21:11.060 --> 00:21:12.060
Ok.


00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:14.440
You got to have
a Williston address.


00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:16.350
BATTEN:
That's my little 2-year-old.


00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:18.850
Her name's Kileigh
and Taylor,


00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:21.870
and my wife's name is Candace.


00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:26.080
My wife, she thinks I'm
at a church, but I'm homeless.


00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:27.580
I think it embarrasses her
a little bit.


00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:29.080
I don't blame her.


00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:31.580
I mean, she's got
a little pride in her.


00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:33.580
And all my pride's gone.


00:21:33.590 --> 00:21:36.100
My pride's been gone
a long time.


00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:39.670
I wish I ain't got to tell her
I ain't got a job yet.


00:21:39.680 --> 00:21:42.690
Just tell her to hold on.


00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:47.100
But I can tell in her voice,
she's about ready to give up.


00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:52.050
KEEGAN EDWARDS: Eh?


